# DriveLinkUp design system

Goal: a calm, premium Swedish marketplace — think ElevenLabs/Linear restraint
with Airbnb/Treatwell marketplace clarity. Warm bone surfaces, ink type, one
green accent, real imagery, generous space. Never "a legal document with
buttons".

This rule sits under `drivelinkup.mdc` (product honesty) and
`page-backgrounds.mdc` (solid backgrounds). Where they conflict, they win.

## 1. Tokens (use these — never hard-code new hex values in components)

Defined in `src/app/custom-style.css` (ELEVENLABS-STYLE RESTYLE block).

| Role | Token | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Page | `--background` | `#f4f2ed` bone (dark: `#0b0b0a`) |
| Deep surface / panels | `--muted` | `#eae7e0` |
| Card | `--card` | `#ffffff` (dark: `#131312`) |
| Text | `--foreground` / `--dlu-ink` | `#131312` |
| Secondary text | `--muted-foreground` | `#565349` |
| Faint text / meta | `--dlu-ink-faint` | `#8d8a83` (decorative only, never body copy) |
| Accent | `--dlu-green` | `#00a876` |
| Accent tint | `--dlu-green-chip` | `#e4f5ec` |
| Accent text on tint | `--dlu-green-deep` | `#0b6b4a` |
| Hairline | `--border` | `rgba(19,19,18,.09)` |

Rules
- One accent only. Green marks *meaning* (active, confirmed, "free", focus,
  check icons, the dot in an eyebrow). It is not a decoration colour. Never
  more than ~5% of a viewport.
- Primary actions are ink pills (`bg-foreground text-background`), not green.
- Solid backgrounds only (see `page-backgrounds.mdc`). Depth comes from
  white cards on bone + hairline borders + soft shadow, not tints.
- Body text must hit WCAG AA (4.5:1). `--dlu-ink-faint` fails AA on bone —
  use it only for decorative meta ≥ 14px medium or icons.

## 2. Typography

- Display: `font-display` (headings), body: `font-body`. Do not add fonts.
- No-bold policy: headings weight 400, emphasis max 500 (section 15 in
  custom-style.css). Hierarchy comes from size and spacing, not weight.
- Scale (mobile → desktop):
  - Hero H1: `text-[2.6rem] sm:text-6xl xl:text-7xl`, `leading-[1.02]`,
    `tracking-[-0.035em]`, max ~14 words, `text-balance`.
  - Section H2: `text-h2` (`text-3xl` on mobile), `tracking-tight`.
  - Lead: `text-body-lg`, `max-w-xl`, `text-muted-foreground`.
  - Eyebrow: `text-eyebrow` — max 3 words, sentence case in the source string.
- Line length 45–75 characters for paragraphs.

## 3. Layout and spacing

- Container: `container-page` (max-w-screen-xl, px-4 sm:px-6).
- 8-pt spacing. Section padding: `py-16 sm:py-24`. Hero: `pt-10 pb-16
  sm:pt-16 lg:pt-20 lg:pb-24`.
- No dead bands: a section never has more than one empty viewport-third.
  If something feels empty, add a visual or tighten — don't add a border.
- Desktop hero: two columns `lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_0.95fr]`, copy left,
  visual right, vertically centred. Mobile: one column, copy → search →
  visual (visual may be shortened/cropped, never removed entirely).
- Cards in a row share height (`grid auto-rows-fr` or `h-full`). Never
  stagger cards with `nth-child` offsets.

## 4. Radius, borders, elevation

- Cards / panels: `rounded-[1.25rem]` (20px). Inputs inside cards: `rounded-xl`.
- Buttons, chips, pills: `rounded-full`.
- Border: `border border-border` (1px hairline). Never two borders stacked.
- Shadows (light mode): resting card `shadow-[0_1px_2px_rgba(19,19,18,.04),0_8px_24px_-12px_rgba(19,19,18,.12)]`;
  raised (search, floating chips) `shadow-[0_2px_4px_rgba(19,19,18,.04),0_24px_48px_-20px_rgba(19,19,18,.22)]`.
  Dark mode: no shadows; rely on borders.

## 5. Components

Buttons
- Primary: ink pill, `h-12 px-6` (hero) / `h-10 px-5` (sections), `→` or a
  lucide icon at `size-4`. One primary per view.
- Secondary: white pill with hairline border, or a plain text link with
  arrow. Never two equally-weighted buttons side by side in a hero.

Search (marketplace entry point)
- The hero leads with search, not a CTA. Fields: körkortskategori + stad,
  then an ink "Sök" button. Native `<select>` is fine and accessible; label
  every field (visible label or `sr-only`).
- Submits to `/instructors` with only the non-empty params (`categories`,
  `city`). Never send empty params — the parser rejects `city=`.
- Below the search: popular chips (category + city). These replace any
  separate "quick links" strip.

Chips
- `rounded-full border border-border bg-card px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm`,
  hover `border-foreground/25`. Selected: `bg-foreground text-background`.

Listing cards
- Logo/avatar with a fallback (initials on `--muted`) — never a broken
  `<img>` or visible alt text.
- Hide empty fields. "Ingen kommande tid listad" etc. is not shown; omit the
  row instead.
- Price is the most prominent data point after the name. Category chips are
  labelled (`Bil · B`, not `B`).

Icons
- lucide-react only, `size-4` inline / `size-5` in feature lists,
  `strokeWidth={1.75}`. Check marks for assurances use `--dlu-green`.

## 6. Imagery

- Every marketing section above the fold needs a visual: product UI,
  photography or illustration. Text-only heroes are not allowed.
- Illustration style: flat editorial, top-down or 3/4, warm bone palette,
  ink linework, one green accent, lots of negative space. No clip-art, no
  gradients-as-background, no stock "thumbs up" photos.
- Images via `next/image` with explicit `sizes`, `priority` only for the
  hero image, meaningful Swedish `alt` from i18n.
- Floating overlay chips on imagery may only state **product facts that are
  true for every listing** (e.g. "Priset i kronor före bokning"). Never
  invented ratings, counts, slots, names or prices — see `drivelinkup.mdc`.

## 7. Content and honesty in UI

- All strings through `messages/sv.json` + `messages/en.json` (rule 19).
  Add both languages in the same change.
- Legal/compliance clarifications (e.g. "en katalogannons är inte ett
  bokningstillstånd") are shown **once per page**, as a small footnote near
  the relevant action — not repeated in the lead paragraph, a card and the
  footer. Never delete them; relocate and shorten only with the same meaning.
- Lead copy says what the learner gets, in their words, before it says what
  DriveLinkUp is not.
- No fake social proof. Show numbers only when computed from the database.

## 8. Motion

- Entrance: existing `marketplace-reveal` (rise 700ms, ease-out-expo),
  staggered 60–120ms. Hover: `-translate-y-0.5` + shadow step, 200ms.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (no transforms, no loops).
- No auto-rotating carousels in the hero.

## 9. Mobile (390px first)

- Tap targets ≥ 44px. Search fields stack full-width; button full-width.
- H1 ≤ 4 lines at 390px. No horizontal scroll at 320px.
- The cookie banner must not cover more than ~25% of the first viewport.

## 10. Mobbin reference workflow (Cursor + Mobbin MCP)

Before redesigning any screen or section:
1. Use the Mobbin MCP to pull 4–8 references from shipped products in the
   same pattern. Default reference set for DriveLinkUp: Airbnb, Booking.com,
   Treatwell, Doctolib, Thumbtack, Zocdoc, Linear (type/spacing), ElevenLabs
   (bone/ink restraint).
2. Summarise the shared pattern (layout, hierarchy, what's above the fold,
   how search/CTA is handled, how trust is shown) in 5–8 bullets.
3. Map it onto these tokens. Do not copy another product's colours or fonts.
4. Implement one section per change.
5. Run the method in §12 and name the Laws of UX (§13) that drove each
   decision.

## 11. QA checklist (every UI change)

- [ ] Screenshot at 390 × 844 and 1440 × 900, light and dark.
- [ ] No overflow, clipped text, broken images, or alt text visible.
- [ ] Contrast AA on all text; focus rings visible on every control.
- [ ] Strings exist in sv and en; no raw i18n keys rendered.
- [ ] No fabricated data introduced; compliance copy still present once.
- [ ] `npm run typecheck && npx biome check` clean for touched files.
- [ ] Laws of UX (§13) named; POV statement (§12) still holds; the
      journey stages before and after are unbroken.

## 12. Design method: Stanford design thinking for service design

Every design iteration runs the d.school's five modes
(Empathize → Define → Ideate → Prototype → Test, see
https://dschool.stanford.edu/tools/design-thinking-bootleg), applied to the
**whole service**, not just one screen. DriveLinkUp is a service with
several actors: learner, supervisor (handledare), driving school, instructor
and DriveLinkUp itself.

1. **Empathize.** Name the actor and the journey stage before touching the UI:
   discover → compare → choose → book → pay → lesson → after the lesson.
   Base this on evidence in the repo (`docs/`, specs, ADRs, support copy) and
   the Mobbin references. Never invent users, quotes, interviews or research
   results. If evidence is missing, write it down as an assumption to test.
2. **Define.** Write one point-of-view statement and one "How might we…?"
   question per section, e.g. "A learner in Göteborg needs to see the total
   price before choosing a school, because hidden fees break trust."
3. **Ideate.** Sketch at least 3 distinct directions (in words or ASCII)
   before choosing one. Pick using the Laws of UX in §13, and say which law
   decided it.
4. **Prototype.** Build the smallest change that can be tested: one section
   per change, behind real data only, and reversible.
5. **Test.** Check the result against the POV statement, the §11 QA
   checklist and the §13 laws. Write down what you learned and what would
   send you back to Define. Prototype as if you're right; test as if you're
   wrong.

Service-design checks for every iteration:
- **Journey map.** Which stage does this change affect, and what happens in the
  stage just before and just after? The hand-offs (search → profile →
  booking → confirmation e-mail) must not break.
- **Front stage / back stage.** What the user sees has to match what the
  system actually does (booking rules, verification, consent). The UI never
  promises something the back end doesn't do.
- **Every actor.** A change for learners must not make things worse for schools,
  and the other way round. Check the school-facing pages too (`/for-skolor`,
  dashboard).
- **Touchpoints.** Copy and tone match across web, e-mail and SMS.

## 13. Laws of UX (https://lawsofux.com) — apply and cite

Name the laws you applied in every plan and every report. The ones that matter
most for DriveLinkUp:

- **Jakob's Law:** search, filters, cards and checkout work like Airbnb or
  Booking.com. Don't reinvent familiar patterns.
- **Hick's Law / Choice Overload:** at most 1 primary CTA per view. Keep
  filters progressive (the essentials first, "fler filter" for the rest).
  Results must be sortable.
- **Fitts's Law:** primary actions are big and close to the thumb. Tap targets are at least
  44 px, and the search button is full width on mobile.
- **Miller's Law / Chunking / Cognitive Load:** group the information on a
  card as price · category · city · language. Show at most about 5 facts per card.
  Split long forms into steps.
- **Law of Proximity / Common Region / Similarity / Uniform Connectedness:**
  related controls sit together in one bordered surface (like the hero
  search). The same kinds of things look the same.
- **Von Restorff Effect:** only one element per view gets the accent or ink
  emphasis. That is the primary action.
- **Serial Position Effect:** the most important item comes first or last
  in lists, navigation and footers.
- **Aesthetic-Usability Effect:** visual polish matters, but it never hides
  a usability or honesty problem.
- **Doherty Threshold:** respond in under 400 ms. Show a skeleton or optimistic
  state for search and booking, never a blank screen.
- **Goal-Gradient / Zeigarnik:** show the progress in multi-step flows
  (signup, booking, verification) and let users pick up where they left off.
- **Peak-End Rule:** make the booking confirmation and the "after the
  lesson" moment clear and calm. Those are what users remember.
- **Tesler's Law:** complexity that can't be removed (ID checks, supervisor
  rules) is handled by the system and explained once, not pushed onto the
  user.
- **Postel's Law:** accept flexible input (city spelling, phone formats,
  å/ä/ö). Output exact, consistent formats (SEK, dates in sv-SE).
- **Paradox of the Active User / Mental Model:** no instructions needed. Labels
  use the learner's own words ("körkort B", "pris per lektion").
- **Occam's Razor / Prägnanz:** when two designs work equally well, pick the
  simpler one.
